WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:04.800
Now, you might ask.

00:04.840 --> 00:05.360
You know what?

00:05.400 --> 00:08.360
Why do I have to understand the differences between all those stuff?

00:08.400 --> 00:10.680
The nulls, empty strings and the blanks.

00:10.880 --> 00:11.440
Everything.

00:11.440 --> 00:12.280
Like empty.

00:12.280 --> 00:13.520
So why do I care?

00:13.560 --> 00:18.520
Well, in real projects, I'm going to promise you that you will be working with sources and data that

00:18.520 --> 00:19.920
has bad data quality.

00:20.240 --> 00:23.440
And you might encounter all those three scenarios in your data.

00:23.480 --> 00:29.280
And now, if you don't do any data preparations like cleaning up the data, handling those three scenarios

00:29.280 --> 00:34.800
and bringing standards to your data, and you jump immediately to the analysis without doing all those

00:34.800 --> 00:41.760
stuff, you will end up providing inaccurate results in your reports and analyses, which leads to wrong

00:41.760 --> 00:42.480
decisions.

00:42.560 --> 00:47.800
So preparing your data before doing an analysis by cleaning up the data, handling those three scenarios

00:48.040 --> 00:53.840
and as well bringing standards is a very important step before doing any analysis.

00:53.920 --> 00:59.160
So this is how we're going to do it together with the stakeholders and the users of your reports and

00:59.160 --> 01:03.240
analyses, you have to define a clear data policies.

01:03.240 --> 01:08.710
It's like rules and you have to commit yourself during the implementations by following those rules.

01:08.750 --> 01:10.710
And here we have three different options.

01:10.830 --> 01:14.190
The first one you can go and define the data policies like this.

01:14.230 --> 01:19.390
Only use nulls and empty string but avoid using blank spaces.

01:19.390 --> 01:25.110
In my projects, I cannot imagine that there is a scenario where we need blank spaces.

01:25.110 --> 01:26.270
They are just evil.

01:26.270 --> 01:27.670
Just go get rid of them.

01:27.670 --> 01:32.270
All right, so with this policy, we have to go and get rid of all blank spaces inside our data.

01:32.270 --> 01:37.390
And in order to do that, we have a wonderful function in SQL called Trim, the trim function in SQL

01:37.430 --> 01:43.670
going to go and remove the spaces from a string from the left side, and as well from the right side.

01:43.670 --> 01:47.470
So all the leading spaces and the trailing spaces is going to be removed.

01:47.470 --> 01:50.830
So now if you go and apply that trim function on that category, what's going to happen?

01:51.190 --> 01:56.590
All the blank space is going to be removed and it's going to be turned into empty string.

01:56.590 --> 01:57.750
So let's go and do that.

01:57.750 --> 01:58.830
It's very simple.

01:58.830 --> 02:03.350
So we're going to use the trim function and we're going to apply it on the category.

02:04.190 --> 02:06.950
Let's go and call it policy one.

02:06.950 --> 02:08.910
So let's go and execute it.

02:08.910 --> 02:14.750
So now by just comparing the policy one with the category, you see like it's identical but it's not.

02:14.790 --> 02:19.390
Now in order to have a better feeling about this, we can go and test it using the data links.

02:19.430 --> 02:22.230
Now let's go again and use the data link function.

02:22.990 --> 02:25.950
So we're going to use it for the whole results.

02:25.950 --> 02:30.070
And as well I'm going to go and use it for the category in order to just compare it.

02:30.430 --> 02:32.270
So without the trim.

02:36.310 --> 02:38.630
So like this let's go and execute it.

02:38.670 --> 02:42.590
Now if you go and check the result, as you can see here again we have the links of two because here

02:42.590 --> 02:43.830
we have two spaces.

02:43.830 --> 02:46.910
But with the policy one we have zero.

02:46.910 --> 02:52.590
So those two values after applying the trim function they have the links of zero.

02:52.710 --> 02:55.230
And with that we don't have blank spaces.

02:55.230 --> 03:01.150
So that means now we are sure after applying the trim we have either a null or empty string.

03:01.150 --> 03:03.910
So let me just get rid of all those informations.

03:03.950 --> 03:06.990
Now I am sure both of them are empty string.

03:06.990 --> 03:08.710
So as you can see it's very simple.

03:08.750 --> 03:14.060
Using only one SQL function you are cleaning up the data and bringing standards.

03:14.100 --> 03:14.460
All right.

03:14.460 --> 03:15.780
Moving on to the option two.

03:15.820 --> 03:18.020
You can define your data policies like this.

03:18.060 --> 03:23.660
Only use nulls and avoid both empty strings and as well blank spaces.

03:23.660 --> 03:28.780
So that means in our business we don't have anything meaningful for the empty string and the blank spaces.

03:29.020 --> 03:31.100
We can go and use only the nulls.

03:31.140 --> 03:32.860
Okay, so now let's go and implement this rule.

03:32.860 --> 03:36.300
We have to go and convert a value to a null.

03:36.460 --> 03:38.900
So the value is going to be empty string to a null.

03:38.940 --> 03:44.260
And as we learned we can go and use the null if function in order to get nulls instead of values.

03:44.260 --> 03:46.340
So let's go and apply this policy.

03:46.340 --> 03:49.820
But now here we have two values the empty string and spaces.

03:49.860 --> 03:57.100
Now instead of having two rules for that, I'm going to convert first the blank spaces to an empty string

03:57.100 --> 03:58.300
like we have done here.

03:58.300 --> 04:02.620
So I'm going to take the result of this function first as a first step.

04:02.820 --> 04:05.540
And afterwards we're going to go and use the null.

04:05.580 --> 04:11.420
If so we're going to say null if for the result of the trim.

04:11.860 --> 04:15.940
If you find any empty strings convert it to null.

04:15.940 --> 04:17.020
So that's it.

04:17.060 --> 04:17.860
Policy two.

04:18.100 --> 04:23.980
So as you can see in the results, we have converted those empty spaces and blanks to null so that we

04:24.020 --> 04:25.060
are getting three nulls.

04:25.060 --> 04:26.660
And of course we're going to get the value a.

04:26.660 --> 04:30.820
And now if you compare those three columns side by side you're going to see the policy.

04:30.860 --> 04:34.700
Two is really easier to understand compared to the previous ones.

04:34.700 --> 04:34.980
Right.

04:35.020 --> 04:40.100
So now if you compare the policy two now to the policy one, you can see it's easier to understand and

04:40.100 --> 04:41.580
it's easier as well to handle.

04:41.580 --> 04:45.380
So again, it's very easy to do data clean up with only two functions.

04:45.380 --> 04:47.940
We have now like standards inside our data.

04:47.980 --> 04:52.180
And now moving on to the last option we can define our data policy like this.

04:52.220 --> 04:59.700
Use only a default value unknown and avoid using anything else like nulls, empty strings and blank

04:59.700 --> 05:00.420
spaces.

05:00.420 --> 05:06.020
So that means in the analysis and reports, we want to see the value unknown, and we have to handle

05:06.020 --> 05:09.580
all those three informations and convert them to unknown.

05:09.580 --> 05:14.980
So now in order to implement the policy three, we have to go and convert a null with a value a default

05:14.980 --> 05:15.260
value.

05:15.260 --> 05:16.580
And here we have two options.

05:16.780 --> 05:20.220
Either use the is null or we can go and use the coalesce.

05:20.260 --> 05:21.900
And I will go with the coalesce.

05:21.940 --> 05:23.140
So coalesce.

05:24.500 --> 05:27.020
And I'm going to use directly the category.

05:27.020 --> 05:31.460
So if you find any null replace it with the default value unknown.

05:31.980 --> 05:33.940
And let's call it policy three.

05:33.940 --> 05:35.380
So let's go and execute it.

05:35.380 --> 05:39.580
So now if you check the result over here you see that we got it only once correct.

05:39.580 --> 05:42.140
So we replace the null with the unknown.

05:42.300 --> 05:45.220
But we still have like empty spaces and blanks.

05:45.220 --> 05:49.380
And that's because we rushed using the coalesce and we skipped the other steps.

05:49.380 --> 05:53.460
So as you can see preparing the data you have to do it slowly step by step.

05:53.460 --> 05:56.980
So first we have to go and convert everything to a null like the policy two.

05:57.380 --> 06:00.940
And after that the last step we're going to go and use the default value.

06:00.940 --> 06:05.860
So that means instead of using the category we have to go and get the result of the policy two.

06:05.900 --> 06:10.860
So let's go and copy it and replace the category with those two steps.

06:10.860 --> 06:12.820
And let's go and execute it.

06:12.820 --> 06:17.180
So now as you can see we have the default value for all the three scenarios.

06:17.220 --> 06:21.460
First we have to trim the data in order to remove all the blank spaces.

06:21.500 --> 06:26.450
The second step we're going to go and replace all the empty strings with a null.

06:26.490 --> 06:29.850
And with that, we're going to get a null for all those three scenarios.

06:29.850 --> 06:35.490
And finally we're going to go and replace the nulls with a default value the unknown.

06:35.490 --> 06:36.970
So that's it for the three processes.

06:36.970 --> 06:42.770
And this is the different ways in order to clean up the data and bring standards before doing analysis.

06:42.810 --> 06:46.050
And now you might ask me okay, which one should I use in my project.

06:46.090 --> 06:50.090
Like if I want to suggest something for the users, which one should I use?

06:50.090 --> 06:55.330
Well, it really depends on the business, but I try always to avoid this one, the policy one because

06:55.330 --> 06:58.650
it's always confusing and I have always to explain it for the users.

06:58.650 --> 07:00.770
So now we are left with the two and three.

07:00.810 --> 07:03.170
Well, I use both of them in different scenarios.

07:03.210 --> 07:08.890
I normally go with the policy two because it takes less storage and as well the performance of your

07:08.890 --> 07:11.010
queries afterwards can be really good.

07:11.010 --> 07:18.130
So if I'm doing data preparations in my ETL before inserting it inside a table, I go with the policy

07:18.170 --> 07:18.490
two.

07:18.530 --> 07:24.290
But in other hand, if I'm doing a preparation step before showing it in a report like in Tableau or

07:24.290 --> 07:24.970
Power BI.

07:25.170 --> 07:31.440
So if it is like one of the last steps before showing the data to the users, I go with the policy three

07:31.440 --> 07:35.680
because if you present a null insider report, it's going to be really hard to read.

07:35.840 --> 07:39.640
So having like a word like unknown, it's easier to understand.

07:39.680 --> 07:41.240
Okay, we have here missing data.

07:41.240 --> 07:47.080
So again if the data preparations is exactly before I present the data to the users, I go with the

07:47.080 --> 07:49.360
policy three where I use default values.

07:49.560 --> 07:55.760
But if I'm using a data preparations before inserting it in the database, I go with the policy two

07:55.800 --> 08:00.520
because it's going to optimize the storage and it's going to be really bad if you go with the policy.

08:00.520 --> 08:03.960
Three because it's really bad to store the whole world each time.

08:03.960 --> 08:06.000
There is no value like the unknown.

08:06.040 --> 08:07.480
It's going to take a lot of space.

08:07.480 --> 08:11.000
And as well you're going to get bad performance as you are building the queries.

08:11.000 --> 08:13.800
That's why I tend to store the data using nulls.

08:13.800 --> 08:17.160
If you present it to the users, go and show it as a default value.

08:17.160 --> 08:21.640
So as you can see, it's very important to understand the differences between the nulls, empty strings

08:21.640 --> 08:27.080
and blanks, and how to prepare the data by cleaning up the data and bringing standards and policies

08:27.080 --> 08:28.920
before doing any analyses.

08:28.920 --> 08:33.720
So with this, we have clear up the confusion between those scenarios and if you encounter it in your

08:33.720 --> 08:35.760
projects, you know how to deal with it.
